WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.093
- [Interviewer] Alright.

00:00:02.093 --> 00:00:04.675
So Lima, Lima Jevremovic,

00:00:04.675 --> 00:00:05.550
- Jevremovic.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.269
- [Interviewer] You have a company

00:00:07.269 --> 00:00:08.102
- Yes.

00:00:08.102 --> 00:00:09.235
- [Interviewer] called Aura.

00:00:09.235 --> 00:00:10.860
And what do you guys do?

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.990
- [Lima] So we're all
about data-driven healing.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.850
Our goal is to use technology

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.283
to scale affordable high
quality treatment services.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:21.570
- [Interviewer] How'd
you start doing this?

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.116
What got you into this in the first place?

00:00:24.116 --> 00:00:28.800
- So my, our father
growing up was very abusive

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.720
to my sisters and I,

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.540
and my mom left when I
was about five years old

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.840
and my sisters were
around three years old,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.840
and I remembered everything,
absolutely everything.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.410
So growing up, there
was nothing that my mom

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.928
could do to make me feel better.

00:00:44.928 --> 00:00:49.470
I was a miserable child and
very angry all the time.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.450
And dealing with all of
that throughout my life

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.950
and learning how to cope is what initially

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.273
made me want to go into mental health.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.550
- [Interviewer] So, what do you
think causes these problems?

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.660
Why do you think this
exists in our society?

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.210
- Yeah, so it's not about a housing issue.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.630
It initially started
with abolishing slavery

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.580
and trying to get cheap labor.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.860
And that's when we began to
try to fill our prison systems,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.730
and we closed mental
health facilities to open

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:19.940
up more prisons.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.050
A lot of the funding
cuts ended up, you know,

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.508
being taken out of mental health.

00:01:24.508 --> 00:01:26.880
And so that's why you see so many people

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.150
on the streets with mental health issues.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:32.236
Another problem is that
when you look at the numbers

00:01:32.236 --> 00:01:35.609
of people who are imprisoned,

00:01:35.609 --> 00:01:38.080
roughly about 60% of them have,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.310
are in there for substance
use related charges,

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.199
and roughly about the same amount, 60%,

00:01:45.199 --> 00:01:47.747
also have diagnosed
mental health conditions

00:01:47.747 --> 00:01:52.463
with about 20% having severe
illnesses like schizophrenia.

00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:54.840
So it's all about,

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.960
right now, our system is about
punishment over treatment,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.610
and really monetizing that labor.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.970
And they're taking all of the funding away

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.780
from mental health services

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.727
and putting it into other resources,

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:10.730
which is completely damaging our society.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.050
And so if we put more services,

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.930
more funding behind
mental health services,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.480
we wouldn't have such a
horrific mental health

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.343
and homeless situation
happening in our country.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.840
- [Interviewer] So what
you're suggesting is the

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.860
appropriate response is more treatment

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.650
for the mental health issues?

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.510
- Absolutely. It's all about
treatment over punishment

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.716
and really learning how to help people.

00:02:35.716 --> 00:02:38.140
- [Interviewer] What
got you into this field?

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.340
I mean, your, your, your
history with your family,

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:41.480
it sounds like.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.737
- Yeah. So, like I said, I
initially didn't remember,

00:02:44.737 --> 00:02:47.257
my sisters didn't remember
any of the trauma,

00:02:47.257 --> 00:02:51.010
but I had a, you know,
I remembered everything.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.200
And throughout my childhood and
throughout my teenage years,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.670
I just got mixed up with the wrong crowd.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:02.310
And my sisters the poster
children for the perfect child.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.030
They were identical twins.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.500
They were athletes.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.300
They had straight A's.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.290
They had no recollection of the trauma.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.580
And then roughly within
their mid twenties,

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.547
they began to hear voices.

00:03:15.547 --> 00:03:20.480
And they didn't know what was going on,

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.290
but because of the mental health stigma,

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.750
they didn't come to my mom or my myself.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:26.790
They kept it a secret,

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.280
but they did do the right thing,

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.300
and they sought professional help.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.850
And so the doctors, of course,

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:33.730
had absolutely no tools to treat them,

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.410
their mental health therapists.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.780
So they go and they ask
them, how are you feeling?

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.650
And then they started prescribing them

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.640
cocktails of medications and drugs.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.160
And so at that time,

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.109
my sisters had a very
successful YouTube channel.

00:03:48.109 --> 00:03:51.720
Their career was on the
up and up and rising.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.636
And once they started to seek treatment

00:03:54.636 --> 00:03:57.240
is when it all went downhill from there.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.450
So they began, at the beginning,

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.520
they were prescribed antipsychotics,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.153
misdiagnosed for about three years.

00:04:05.153 --> 00:04:07.240
They were told that
they were schizophrenic,

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.830
which is not the case.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.510
And it's the, the way that
we treat mental health

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.320
services right now is the equivalent

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.970
of me telling you, Mark, my leg hurts.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:18.060
I think it's broken.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.384
And then you ask me, well,

00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:23.366
on a scale of one to
10, how bad is the pain?

00:04:23.366 --> 00:04:27.202
And I look at you, and I
tell you, maybe it's a seven.

00:04:27.202 --> 00:04:29.820
And so then you tell me, Oh,
it's probably not broken.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:30.770
It's probably fractured.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:31.897
Take this medication.

00:04:31.897 --> 00:04:34.780
And if it gets worse,

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:37.430
then come back, and
we'll put a cast on it.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.160
That's basically what we do to your brain.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.040
We're playing Russian
roulette with your brain.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.370
Every time we prescribed you medications,

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.160
we're slightly changing
the chemicals in the brain,

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.990
when we really don't
know what the issue is.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.640
And so after three and a half years,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.490
my sisters were finally properly diagnosed

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.290
with dissociative identity disorder,

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:59.350
which is extremely dangerous
to treat with antipsychotics.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.420
Because in that three-year period

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.800
the anti-psychotics were not working,

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.490
the doctors were giving them cocktails

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:09.470
of antipsychotics, benzos,
so Klonopin and Xanax,

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:10.850
and eventually,

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:14.860
because the Klonopin destroys
the reward receptors,

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:16.554
somebody prescribed them Suboxone,

00:05:16.554 --> 00:05:19.070
which is synthetic opioid,

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:20.650
and they ended up developing

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:23.991
a full blown opioid and benzo addiction

00:05:23.991 --> 00:05:27.500
that's completely destroyed our family.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.339
So, but initially,

00:05:29.339 --> 00:05:33.270
the cycle is that they go
to treatment for 30 days

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.523
because that's what insurance will cover.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:40.380
The first approximately
10 days is horrible

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.280
'cause you're just detoxing your body.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:45.063
After that,

00:05:45.063 --> 00:05:47.020
when the drugs are completely
out of your system,

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:49.160
and they're trying to
treat you with therapy,

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:50.890
for about two weeks, your brain,

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.120
essentially medications are like

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:54.630
putting your brain in a wheelchair.

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:58.110
So if I put you in a
wheelchair for three years,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.240
and then I ask you to stand up, you're,

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.652
you're not going to be able to walk,

00:06:01.652 --> 00:06:05.660
nonetheless be able to even
stand on, on your legs.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:09.681
And so if you've been taking
meds or self-medicating

00:06:09.681 --> 00:06:12.970
or very dependent on
different types of medications

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:16.370
and then they're taken away
from you and you've detoxed,

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.770
your brain doesn't know
how to manually produce

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.050
the chemicals on its own.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:22.460
It's very dependent

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.500
and has been essentially
crippled by this medication.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.810
So during that detox period,

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.120
as soon as your body's
completely kept cleansed

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:31.660
of those chemicals,

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.700
it doesn't know how to
produce it on its own,

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.440
and it's very much depleted,

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.490
so you just feel awful all the time.

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:38.418
And a lot of times,

00:06:38.418 --> 00:06:41.530
those people need to sit
in that awful feeling,

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:44.300
and it's too overwhelming
for them to do so.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:48.200
And so initially after detox,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.040
the following about two weeks

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.460
is the brain learning how to function

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:54.690
without that medication.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.800
So you are just hating life.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:58.350
Therapy's not getting through to you.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.250
Nothing's really making sense.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:01.400
You just want to leave.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:04.417
And then your brain is now
starting to produce its own

00:07:04.417 --> 00:07:08.730
chemicals and function on its own.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:11.230
It's, you're learning how to
stand up, let's say, right,

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.063
all over again.

00:07:12.063 --> 00:07:14.250
That physical therapy's
starting to have an effect.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.900
So the last week of treatment,

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.963
the last week of that 30 days,

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:20.490
you're starting to benefit,

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.910
and you're starting to seem
like you're getting better.

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.540
And then you're immediately released

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:27.635
because insurance won't
cover continued care.

00:07:27.635 --> 00:07:29.480
The reason for that is

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.990
because it costs about $45,000 per month

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:35.980
for somebody to get treatment in resident,

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:37.630
in a residential facility.

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:39.606
That doesn't include the
initial hospitalization,

00:07:39.606 --> 00:07:42.882
the detox period, and
all of the medications

00:07:42.882 --> 00:07:45.539
and everything else that goes into that.

00:07:45.539 --> 00:07:50.539
And it's, it's very expensive,
but the relapse rate is 90%.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:53.307
So with a 10% success rate,

00:07:53.307 --> 00:07:57.370
the insurance companies
are saying, we are not,

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.350
we're not going to fund 45, 45, 45,

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:02.246
when you have such a high relapse rate.

00:08:02.246 --> 00:08:04.900
The clinics, it's a catch 22,

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.493
because the clinics are
initially saying, well,

00:08:09.215 --> 00:08:14.215
on average, most patients
have been self-medicating

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:18.770
or initially addicted
or harming themselves

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:20.330
for 15 years or more.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:22.450
They've been, they've been taking this,

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.400
these matters into their own hands.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:29.400
And you can't expect us to change 15 years

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:32.289
of a behavior pattern in only 30 days

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:34.010
and then expect it to last.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.150
And so with my sisters' case,

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.220
they've been to over 60 treatment centers,

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:41.040
and the story's always the same.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:43.143
It's 30 days of treatment.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:47.770
You know, they leave, there's
no door to door treat,

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.040
there's no door to door care.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.190
There's such a high
demand for these services,

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:53.290
that there's not enough doctors to treat

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.610
the number of patients
that needs, needs help.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.572
Especially since the opioid epidemic,

00:08:57.572 --> 00:09:00.118
there's been a 600% increase

00:09:00.118 --> 00:09:03.897
in the number of patients
trying to admit themselves

00:09:03.897 --> 00:09:05.345
into treatment.

00:09:05.345 --> 00:09:08.390
So there's a nationwide
shortage of beds and doctors

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:10.270
and staff and support and help.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:12.692
And so they leave the 30 day treatment.

00:09:12.692 --> 00:09:15.720
They feel suddenly like
they're on their own.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.470
A lot of these people, as you know,

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.170
have this story that they tell themselves

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:20.530
that nobody cares about me.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:21.420
I'm by myself.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:23.750
Nobody, nobody wants to help me.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:26.580
So when they're starting
to get better in a facility

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.442
and then they find
themselves abandoned again,

00:09:29.442 --> 00:09:32.830
then they just feel like
I'm completely alone

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:34.583
with my illness or my addiction,

00:09:34.583 --> 00:09:36.537
And the only thing that they know,

00:09:36.537 --> 00:09:40.100
because the thing that makes
them feel better is an instant

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.080
immediate satisfaction,

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:45.175
whether that be cutting, whether
it be high risk behaviors,

00:09:45.175 --> 00:09:47.293
being promiscuous,

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:51.239
compulsive caffeine consumption,

00:09:51.239 --> 00:09:54.180
shopping addiction like me, right?

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.073
So there's, there's so
many different ways.

00:09:56.073 --> 00:09:57.940
And these are very common.

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:01.390
These are what I call
negative coping skills.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:02.467
So they're very common,

00:10:02.467 --> 00:10:06.948
but addiction is not just an
addiction to drugs or alcohol.

00:10:06.948 --> 00:10:11.020
It's initially the
inability to say no. Right?

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:13.030
So it's, it's the,

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:16.310
it's the inability to set
those boundaries for yourself.

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.708
And I noticed that a lot of times you ask,

00:10:18.708 --> 00:10:20.106
I mean, I think every time,

00:10:20.106 --> 00:10:22.418
you ask the people that
you're interviewing,

00:10:22.418 --> 00:10:23.616
tell me about your childhood.

00:10:23.616 --> 00:10:24.982
- [Interviewer] Every time.

00:10:24.982 --> 00:10:26.858
- It really does start in childhood

00:10:26.858 --> 00:10:29.960
because you need a healthy balance.

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:32.524
So if, if someone comes from a place

00:10:32.524 --> 00:10:35.188
where there's too many boundaries, right,

00:10:35.188 --> 00:10:37.830
where the parents are very,
very strict with them,

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:40.875
and they're, you know,
can be also abusive in

00:10:40.875 --> 00:10:42.536
in how strict they are,

00:10:42.536 --> 00:10:45.031
that can be completely debilitating

00:10:45.031 --> 00:10:47.621
to a child growing into an adult.

00:10:47.621 --> 00:10:49.930
And the same thing can be with no fre-,

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:51.400
with too much freedom,

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:54.018
where parents just allow
you to do whatever you want.

00:10:54.018 --> 00:10:56.950
Children need healthy boundaries

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:58.980
so that they can learn to say no,

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:01.720
and they can, they can
understand their self-worth.

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:03.234
And you have to find that balance.

00:11:03.234 --> 00:11:06.010
But if you have parents
that are too freeing

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:07.852
and are, are too consumed with what

00:11:07.852 --> 00:11:10.530
they're personally involved in

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:12.350
and they don't pay attention to the kids,

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:13.687
or if you have kids that are,

00:11:13.687 --> 00:11:15.793
parents that are too controlling,

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:19.067
you have your boundaries
completely screwed up.

00:11:19.067 --> 00:11:21.750
And so that's, that's where it all starts,

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.777
with not having proper boundaries in place

00:11:24.777 --> 00:11:29.413
and, and becoming addicted
to your own self-destruction.

00:11:29.413 --> 00:11:33.870
And a lot of times when we say treatment,

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.360
people assume that going to therapy is,

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.130
so Mark, tell me about how you're feeling,

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:41.740
and then you just puke
everything out on me.

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:42.573
And then you leave,

00:11:42.573 --> 00:11:44.850
and you feel great after
your therapy session.

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:47.313
That's really not how therapy works.

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:51.670
Therapy starts when you step
outside the door. Right?

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:54.940
So one of therapists, her name is Alana,

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:57.554
and she, she uses our technology.

00:11:57.554 --> 00:11:59.870
She works with veterans.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:01.562
So she always tells her,

00:12:01.562 --> 00:12:04.802
her veterans that therapy is all theory.

00:12:04.802 --> 00:12:08.460
And it starts, the work
starts when you leave.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:10.281
The reason that she loves our technology

00:12:10.281 --> 00:12:14.353
is because she's able to track
how the patients are doing.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:17.470
We use voice analysis.

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:19.500
We use biofeedback.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.930
So you can actually see
how a patient's feeling.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:25.010
I'm not just asking you,
how bad is the pain? Right?

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:28.760
I'm actually, I give the
doctors the tools to see,

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:33.100
so if you tell me that,
you know, you're fine.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:35.970
And I look, and I can
see that you're not fine.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:38.900
Addicts are master manipulators.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:42.120
They have it spot on, on how to trick you.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:46.040
And on top of that, my sisters
are the best actresses.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:48.835
So it's hard enough for me to read them,

00:12:48.835 --> 00:12:52.060
but being able to have
tools that, you know,

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:55.000
when, when someone's been just so,

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:56.600
gone to treatment, it hasn't helped.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:58.010
Gone to treatment, it hasn't helped.

00:12:58.010 --> 00:12:59.610
Gone to treatment, it hasn't helped.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.386
They initially give up on treatment,

00:13:01.386 --> 00:13:03.290
and they feel like they know better.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:06.002
And especially when I
go in to see a doctor,

00:13:06.002 --> 00:13:08.164
and they ask me, how are you feeling?

00:13:08.164 --> 00:13:09.861
And I tell them, I'm fine.

00:13:09.861 --> 00:13:12.170
And they're like, Oh, that's good.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:14.040
Then I feel like I can't trust that doctor

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.840
because they can't see behind the mask,

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:17.130
so they can't help me.

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:20.050
So if it's so easy for me to fool you,

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:22.100
then you can't help me.

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.870
And so that, that
initially discredits a lot

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.283
of the therapists or the therapy sessions,

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:29.780
the ability to just be able to see

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:31.270
how the patient's feeling.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.500
We also use virtual reality,

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:37.660
so we can put patients in
triggering environments.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:41.640
And, you know, for example,

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:44.470
we put a patient in a bar,

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:46.150
or we put, or for our veterans,

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:47.991
we put them in a war zone.

00:13:47.991 --> 00:13:52.991
We've learned a lot about their
response to certain things.

00:13:54.658 --> 00:13:56.121
It just gives us a lot.

00:13:56.121 --> 00:14:00.290
It's all about having
that vast richness of data

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:02.280
to really be able to analyze the situation

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:05.820
and steer the conversation in a path

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:07.670
that it wouldn't have gone otherwise,

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:11.153
just because there's so many
walls around these people

00:14:11.153 --> 00:14:13.930
that they're just not
willing to let someone in.

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:16.511
So you kind of have to find
a way to gain their trust

00:14:16.511 --> 00:14:18.637
and show them that you
actually can help them

00:14:18.637 --> 00:14:20.369
and that you can see how they feel,

00:14:20.369 --> 00:14:23.040
and it's not all in their head.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:24.650
Because especially with my sisters,

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:26.570
there's been so many
times where they've said,

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:27.537
maybe DID isn't real.

00:14:27.537 --> 00:14:29.280
Maybe nobody can help me.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:30.543
I'm just crazy.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:35.333
Because up until now, no one
has been able to help them.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:38.120
- [Interviewer] So, so
your company really gives

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:40.527
the therapist a window into
what's really going on?

00:14:40.527 --> 00:14:41.360
- Exactly.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:42.240
- [Interviewer] With the patient.

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:43.210
- Exactly.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:44.880
So initially, it's kind of like-

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:45.950
- [Interviewer] Not just their words.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:46.783
- Absolutely.

00:14:46.783 --> 00:14:51.560
So, so think of it as like
therapy and medication, right?

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:52.950
You do need both,

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:56.450
and, but you, but you need
medication in moderation, right?

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.210
Not the way that we
currently prescribe it,

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:01.340
where it leads to, you know, addiction,

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:03.127
but, and dependency.

00:15:03.127 --> 00:15:05.430
You need, and you also need therapy.

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:08.610
So right now our system
relies purely on medication

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:10.490
and very little on, on therapy.

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:12.978
Therapy is just there so we say we do it,

00:15:12.978 --> 00:15:17.570
but it's medication to sedate
everybody and manage them.

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:22.200
And the way that I, the
way that I initially

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.299
describe to patients or colleagues

00:15:24.299 --> 00:15:26.820
is it's like a fire alarm, right?

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:29.193
So when I have a really awful feeling,

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:33.040
that means that, and it could
even be a midlife crisis,

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:35.970
whatever it is, doesn't even
have to be an addiction, right?

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:37.730
When I have a bad feeling,

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:40.060
that bad feeling's actually really good.

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:41.570
It's telling me that something's wrong,

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:44.180
and you need to learn to
sit in it and analyze it,

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:46.660
initially track and unpack that feeling.

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:51.660
So if there's a fire going
off and you take medication,

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:53.839
it's initially like me getting up,

00:15:53.839 --> 00:15:56.650
taking the batteries out of the fire alarm

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:57.980
and then going back to sleep.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:00.430
So I'm going back to sleep
in a burning building.

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:03.503
And you know, the side
effects are suicide,

00:16:03.503 --> 00:16:08.503
self-harm, just, you know,
they're all horrific. Right?

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:11.118
Because if you depend on that long enough,

00:16:11.118 --> 00:16:12.750
the building's gonna burn down,

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:14.283
and you have no warning signs.

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:20.000
Therapy is about looking for
where the fire is. Right?

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.830
And so that's, that's what our goal is

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:25.380
is to scale treatment
so that you can learn

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:28.086
how to sit in these bad feelings
and that you're, you're,

00:16:28.086 --> 00:16:31.800
when you refer to that feeling,
it's not, I feel awful.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.400
'Cause I hear that from
my sisters all the time.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:34.233
I feel awful.

00:16:34.233 --> 00:16:35.066
I feel awful.

00:16:35.066 --> 00:16:37.440
It's about being aware of your feelings.

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:39.730
So, is it sad?

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:40.670
Is it fear?

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:41.503
Is it anger?

00:16:41.503 --> 00:16:42.980
What is that feeling?

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:45.030
And then let's analyze
where it's coming from.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:47.858
And once you can identify
what triggered that feeling,

00:16:47.858 --> 00:16:51.021
you can identify how to
initially respond to it,

00:16:51.021 --> 00:16:55.140
or you can identify, you know,

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:57.650
how to avoid certain things that end up

00:16:57.650 --> 00:16:58.680
leading to that feeling.

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:00.500
And it's like a domino effect.

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:02.580
But if you're constantly,
like, I feel awful.

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:03.500
This is a bad feeling.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:05.850
Let me take the batteries
out of the fire alarm.

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:08.080
Then you're constantly
quieting that feeling,

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:10.320
and you're not getting better.

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:12.020
- [Interviewer] So for your,
if a pa-, if the therapist

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:13.480
is speaking with a patient

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:17.091
and there may be some shame or anxiety or,

00:17:17.091 --> 00:17:20.572
or something going on that
they're masking or hiding,

00:17:20.572 --> 00:17:23.790
that therapist kind of
has a window into it?

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:26.820
- Absolutely. So they can, they can see,

00:17:26.820 --> 00:17:28.890
we use evidence-based scales.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:31.110
So depending on what the patient is

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:33.700
diagnosed with,

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:36.660
right now, our biggest
focus is on depression.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:38.420
We are a very young company.

00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:40.850
It's a startup, and I started on my own.

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:42.273
- [Interviewer] How many
years has Aura been around?

00:17:42.273 --> 00:17:43.150
- It's been a year.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:44.840
It's been around for a year,

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:48.359
but I've been doing the research
for, for about seven years.

00:17:48.359 --> 00:17:52.930
So we were, it was registered last April.

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:55.810
We, we managed to raise
a small seed round.

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:59.270
We have 19 clinics that are
currently signed up with us.

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:03.140
We have about 600 clinics
that want to sign up with us,

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:05.990
but we just don't have a big
enough team to support them.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:10.660
And we, we don't have the tools to scale,

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:12.160
scale or technology right now.

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:15.210
So what we're working on is
we're getting ready to open

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:18.400
up our series A with private
investors next month.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:20.390
We've had some really good traction

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:22.620
and a lot of really great conversations.

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:25.660
And there's it, it's amazing to see

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:28.675
how many of these investors have

00:18:28.675 --> 00:18:32.106
experienced mental illness in loved ones,

00:18:32.106 --> 00:18:34.750
and they're very motivated
with the same reasons

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:36.400
that I'm motivated by.

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:39.280
So I couldn't imagine
being in a better industry

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:40.433
because there's so much heart in it.

00:18:40.433 --> 00:18:43.170
It's not all about how
much money we can make.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:45.054
Of course there are those people,

00:18:45.054 --> 00:18:47.940
but those are not the
people we want to work with.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:51.227
It's about the people that
are thinking of how can we

00:18:51.227 --> 00:18:54.889
scale help for so many,
for, for everybody.

00:18:54.889 --> 00:18:59.240
And if you look at the
different tiers of treatment,

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:01.833
so some of the patients that,

00:19:01.833 --> 00:19:04.315
or some of the people
that you've interviewed,

00:19:04.315 --> 00:19:07.084
were talking about
different treatment centers

00:19:07.084 --> 00:19:10.183
that they went to, and they, they were,

00:19:10.183 --> 00:19:13.553
didn't have insurance, they
had Medicaid or Medi-Cal,

00:19:13.553 --> 00:19:18.400
and you know, those
treatment facilities, they,

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:22.340
they typically have very
small rooms, twin beds.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:26.800
It kind of looks like a
nice jail cell. Right?

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:28.410
They're, and they're clients of mine.

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:30.760
So we, we work with them. Right?

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:32.360
So it's not talking bad about them.

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:33.970
It's just giving you an image

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:35.792
of the different tiers of treatment.

00:19:35.792 --> 00:19:38.786
They don't, they have
a small outdoor space.

00:19:38.786 --> 00:19:40.135
It's very closed off.

00:19:40.135 --> 00:19:42.670
There's not really a lot of nature,

00:19:42.670 --> 00:19:45.880
and it's just a very
clinical, hospital vibe.

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:47.820
And then we have,

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:51.380
so those ones go for
about like 18,000 a month

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:53.762
to 35,000 a month.

00:19:53.762 --> 00:19:57.020
Then we have luxury facilities that go

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:02.020
from 45,000 upwards to 135,000 a month.

00:20:02.165 --> 00:20:05.680
There are cash pay private facilities and

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:09.620
beautiful houses on the water

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:11.240
or just in a beautiful neighborhood,

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:15.130
surrounded by, you know,
completely surrounded by nature,

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:17.950
and you don't feel like
you're in a hospital setting.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:21.380
And those, the Medicare
Medi-Cal facilities

00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:22.213
will usually have

00:20:22.213 --> 00:20:26.670
about like a hundred, 200
patients, not a large staff.

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:28.443
It's mostly group therapy.

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:32.080
It's initially a place to
hold your family member

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:34.910
for 30 days until they relapse.

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:37.530
So it's, it's a bit, it's
the glorified babysitter

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:39.257
that's holding them. Right?

00:20:39.257 --> 00:20:41.760
The private pay facilities, they're,

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:44.203
they have a smaller,
they take less people,

00:20:44.203 --> 00:20:46.940
which is why they charge
a higher price point.

00:20:46.940 --> 00:20:49.940
And they also do more one-on-one therapy.

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:52.303
And, and they have,

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:56.620
you know, it's enjoyable to be there.

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:58.800
You actually feel like
people care about you.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:20:59.746
And it's beautiful.

00:20:59.746 --> 00:21:02.086
I would check myself into
one of these facilities

00:21:02.086 --> 00:21:04.350
just for the sake of a vacation,

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:07.520
like it's, it's just
very serene and tranquil.

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:11.390
And so with our technology,
with our virtual reality,

00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:14.240
what we're doing is we're
taking the highest quality

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:17.480
of care, so the beaches
and the beauty of nature

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:19.890
and the mindfulness practices that they do

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:22.130
at these higher tier treatment centers,

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:27.130
and we're providing a virtual
room where these patients can,

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:30.000
in these other facilities,

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.940
can go and can be on a beach

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:33.991
and can be in the mountain tops

00:21:33.991 --> 00:21:37.460
and can do mindfulness
and positive practices

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:39.860
because around like four o'clock,

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.680
the therapist will go home,

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:44.250
and the patients are just
left with the TV room,

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:47.115
and they're just being
held there and medicated.

00:21:47.115 --> 00:21:49.089
And so that's not very practical.

00:21:49.089 --> 00:21:51.870
So with our tools,

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:55.330
we're constantly trying to
replace negative coping skills

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:57.050
with positive coping skills.

00:21:57.050 --> 00:21:59.610
So if you're someone
that's very impulsive,

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:04.610
that's use to, you know, cutting
yourself when you're upset

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:10.040
or throwing up or overeating
or taking your drug of choice,

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:11.480
right?

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:13.660
Those are all negative
coping skills, and you,

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:15.780
and you've gotten used to doing them

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:17.750
because the first time
that you ever do anything

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:19.488
it's very nerve-wracking.

00:22:19.488 --> 00:22:21.980
And then the second time you do it,

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:24.260
it's a little familiar,
but it's still, you know,

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:26.010
it's still makes you a little nervous.

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.310
And then the third time,
fourth time, fifth time,

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:31.340
eventually it becomes
abnormal to not do it.

00:22:31.340 --> 00:22:32.520
So for example,

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.440
if you're someone that goes
to the gym six days a week,

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:38.515
and you miss a day of your workout,

00:22:38.515 --> 00:22:41.230
you're going to feel awful
about yourself. Right?

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:45.260
'Cause you are you're, you
have that, that daily routine,

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:46.373
and you just broke it.

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:50.830
But if you're somebody that,
and I've been there, right.

00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:52.360
So I'll have like that daily routine,

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:54.230
and I'll be like, Oh, I can't
believe I screwed it up.

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:57.036
And then, you know, since my sister drama,

00:22:57.036 --> 00:22:59.990
I haven't been working
out like I was before.

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:02.668
And all of a sudden,

00:23:02.668 --> 00:23:06.211
if I go to the gym once a
week, I'm so proud of myself.

00:23:06.211 --> 00:23:09.030
But the following day that I don't go,

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:12.940
it's not a big deal to me
because I'm used to not going.

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:15.966
So that becomes my
behavior pattern, whatever,

00:23:15.966 --> 00:23:18.280
whatever I'm used to is,

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:23.280
is what I constantly follow and
what I reward, or, you know,

00:23:23.347 --> 00:23:25.330
put myself down for.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:28.950
When it comes to self-harm,
like if someone, for example,

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:32.170
with an eating disorder, if
they're used to, if they,

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:35.160
at the beginning, they
throw up for the first time.

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:36.370
It's kind of scary.

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:39.607
You know, it's a scary thing
to eat, to even comprehend.

00:23:39.607 --> 00:23:41.410
It's very uncomfortable.

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:44.740
I don't think anyone likes
the feeling of puking. Right?

00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:46.220
So it's not a comfortable feeling.

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:47.340
It's not a comfortable act

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:49.194
and it's a little, it's a little scary.

00:23:49.194 --> 00:23:53.760
And so when they begin to
do that on a daily basis,

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:57.210
it begin, it becomes very
uncomfortable for them

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:58.337
not to throw up

00:23:58.337 --> 00:24:00.580
because now they've retained that food,

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:02.380
and it's about control,

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:05.470
and you didn't control
your in, your food intake,

00:24:05.470 --> 00:24:07.310
and you didn't throw up.

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:08.920
And so that becomes the norm,

00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:11.900
so you reprimand yourself
for not doing it.

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:14.690
So you broke that schedule
or that comfort level

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:15.716
that you have created.

00:24:15.716 --> 00:24:19.300
And that became your way of
coping with stress and trauma.

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:22.090
When I feel a certain
way, I respond this way.

00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:23.047
And when I don't do it,

00:24:23.047 --> 00:24:26.110
whether that be pills,
whether that be, you know,

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:29.480
food consumption and just
comfort eating, right,

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:31.600
whatever it is that you do,

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:34.040
therapy is about breaking that pattern.

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:36.080
But if you don't get the therapy,

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:38.560
if you don't get constant therapy,

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:41.540
constant practice at
breaking in that pattern,

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:44.016
then initially, you're never gonna,

00:24:44.016 --> 00:24:45.460
you're gonna ever get a break it,

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:49.240
and you're always going to go
back to what you're used to.

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:52.570
And so that's why the relapse rate is 90%,

00:24:52.570 --> 00:24:54.330
and people are not getting better.

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:57.640
And unfortunately, this isn't, you know,

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:00.280
eating disorders have the
highest rate of mortality

00:25:00.280 --> 00:25:02.842
because, especially when
you pair it with bulimia,

00:25:02.842 --> 00:25:06.700
like anorexia has the
highest rate of mortality,

00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:09.120
and when you pair that with bulimia, your,

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.840
your heart has a higher
rate of just stopping.

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:15.370
Like it just stops out
of, you know, it can't,

00:25:15.370 --> 00:25:17.470
it can't continue to support the body.

00:25:17.470 --> 00:25:18.460
Opioids.

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:21.730
Opioids now kill more,
more Americans than guns

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:22.903
or breast cancer.

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:28.220
And it's just because people
have become so dependent

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:31.180
on impulsively changing
how they're feeling,

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:33.310
and they're very addictive.

00:25:33.310 --> 00:25:36.940
The goal of what we do and
what therapy is supposed to do,

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:39.180
but they don't have the tools to do it,

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:41.470
and that's why we're
building the tools to do it,

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:46.470
is initially to take those
negative coping skills,

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:50.369
get you to pause and not be
so impulsive and replace.

00:25:50.369 --> 00:25:51.900
We're not just telling you,

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:56.067
stop doing drugs, stop
throwing up, stop, you know,

00:25:56.067 --> 00:26:00.200
you're compulsive shopping
habits, Mark. Right?

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:01.600
We're not trying to do that.

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:04.200
We're trying to, we're
not trying to replace them

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:05.591
because you can't just stop

00:26:05.591 --> 00:26:08.047
and not know what to do with yourself.

00:26:08.047 --> 00:26:11.790
You have to replace them
with mindfulness practices,

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:14.057
not being impulsive, taking a deep breath.

00:26:14.057 --> 00:26:16.320
One of the games that we have

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:20.870
is learning how to deal with
anxiety and stress and mood.

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:22.103
So for example,

00:26:22.103 --> 00:26:26.550
we've gamified the fight
or flight response. Right?

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:29.540
So when I'm stressed
out, I'm like very tense

00:26:29.540 --> 00:26:34.540
and in, my shoulders are up,
and I'm holding my breath.

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:37.200
And so I can now see it in colors.

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:38.870
And when I'm calm,

00:26:38.870 --> 00:26:42.490
I'm taking a deep breath
and the colors change and,

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:44.540
and it's, and I have certain tasks.

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:47.760
So for certain tasks, I have
to be super stressed out for.

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:50.190
And for other tasks, I
have to be super calm.

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:53.737
So I learn how to control
the fight or flight response.

00:26:53.737 --> 00:26:56.380
And stress is actually
a really good thing.

00:26:56.380 --> 00:26:59.110
And that's what a lot of
people don't talk about it.

00:26:59.110 --> 00:27:00.712
So if people are interested,

00:27:00.712 --> 00:27:05.070
I would highly recommend the
book, Think Fast and Slow.

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:07.782
It talks about the two brain systems,

00:27:07.782 --> 00:27:11.579
where one is very impulsive
and that can be very good,

00:27:11.579 --> 00:27:16.579
but also one is very focused,
and it, I can break it down.

00:27:16.920 --> 00:27:18.400
I don't know if it would be too long,

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:21.120
but, but yeah, it's a very good book,

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:24.030
and it, it really breaks
down how you need stress.

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:25.330
You need stress to focus.

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:27.348
You need stress to be motivated.

00:27:27.348 --> 00:27:30.660
If you know those people, I'm one of them,

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:35.660
that will usually have like
two weeks to do an essay,

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:38.511
and then the night before the essay's due,

00:27:38.511 --> 00:27:42.960
you end up cramming, and you
just write the whole thing,

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:46.100
and you pull an all nighter
and then you submit it. Right?

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:47.910
That's because those people,

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:51.060
like when you have ADD
or when you're just very

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:53.980
when you're always thinking fast, right,

00:27:53.980 --> 00:27:55.640
you can't sit there and focus.

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.193
But all of a sudden when stress kicks in,

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:00.550
my essay's due tomorrow.

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:02.530
I can't fail this class.

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:06.080
That stress actually gets you
to sit down and hyper-focus

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:08.360
and finish it in one night.

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:11.370
So stress can be very,
very, very productive,

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:14.043
but it's about learning
how to channel it. Right?

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:16.940
- [Interviewer] Has
Aura had success stories

00:28:16.940 --> 00:28:18.370
with some patients?

00:28:18.370 --> 00:28:21.230
- Yeah. So we're actually
piloting it right now.

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:23.414
We're working on getting
the data out there.

00:28:23.414 --> 00:28:25.440
Due to the COVID pandemic,

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:28.047
we've kind of put our research on hold.

00:28:28.047 --> 00:28:29.563
So we're still using it.

00:28:29.563 --> 00:28:31.582
We're still tracking patients,

00:28:31.582 --> 00:28:33.820
but we're not releasing that information

00:28:33.820 --> 00:28:36.511
because a lot of the,

00:28:36.511 --> 00:28:38.780
we don't want our numbers to be skewed.

00:28:38.780 --> 00:28:40.160
A lot of the treatment centers,

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:41.360
they're mental health facilities.

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:44.570
They can support a physical outbreak

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:47.190
like a co, the COVID
pandemic inside the facility

00:28:47.190 --> 00:28:49.260
'cause they don't have the staff to care

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:51.392
for the patients' physical illness.

00:28:51.392 --> 00:28:53.845
So they've changed around their protocols.

00:28:53.845 --> 00:28:55.583
We've been helping them see,

00:28:57.080 --> 00:28:59.226
get, receive PPE supplies.

00:28:59.226 --> 00:29:04.083
And, and so just due
to all of those shifts,

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:07.610
we've paused our, our data collection.

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:12.400
But we have, we have seen in little,

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:15.753
just small instances in therapy sessions,

00:29:15.753 --> 00:29:20.753
where patients weren't even
aware of, of their feelings,

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:23.741
because for example,

00:29:23.741 --> 00:29:27.870
somebody who's at a constant, like,

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:31.573
for let's use a scale one
to 10 for easy math, right?

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:34.720
So if someone's constantly

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:38.540
at a stress level of an eight, right,

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:41.040
and you ask them how they're feeling,

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:42.910
they're gonna think that they're fine

00:29:42.910 --> 00:29:45.610
because they're used to
feeling this way all the time.

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:48.130
So right now, I'm okay. Right?

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:50.828
But the doctor can actually
see that they're not fine,

00:29:50.828 --> 00:29:52.460
and that they're actually at a,

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:55.337
at an elevated stress level
that's being maintained

00:29:55.337 --> 00:29:58.700
just because they're constantly on edge.

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:03.510
And so we've had small
little success stories

00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:07.409
in the sense that we've been
able to identify these factors.

00:30:07.409 --> 00:30:09.682
And we've also been able to

00:30:09.682 --> 00:30:12.920
monitor people's craving levels

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:16.237
and monitor people's stress and anxiety

00:30:16.237 --> 00:30:21.237
or even the ability to
remove triggers. Right?

00:30:22.250 --> 00:30:25.370
When, for example, if you're
in a treatment facility,

00:30:25.370 --> 00:30:29.840
and I put you, I put your drug
of choice in front of you,

00:30:29.840 --> 00:30:31.350
that would be extremely unethical.

00:30:31.350 --> 00:30:32.830
It's not allowed. Right?

00:30:32.830 --> 00:30:34.234
There's no drugs on the premises.

00:30:34.234 --> 00:30:37.310
If I can put that drug of
choice in front of you,

00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:39.340
just the visual of it,

00:30:39.340 --> 00:30:41.680
it's not the same as
me showing you a photo,

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:44.477
but being able to put you in a room,

00:30:44.477 --> 00:30:47.200
through our virtual reality technology,

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:51.810
being able to put you in a
room where you, you know,

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:55.100
you essentially are by yourself. Right?

00:30:55.100 --> 00:30:56.440
So now I'm able to test you

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:58.330
because it's the power of VR

00:30:58.330 --> 00:31:01.387
and it's also, you know,
the power of being silent.

00:31:01.387 --> 00:31:05.436
And so all of a sudden,
you're at a bar by yourself.

00:31:05.436 --> 00:31:07.980
For example, one of our veterans, right,

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:12.580
he said, his rock bottom is
being at home by himself,

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:16.111
no one there, just sitting on the couch

00:31:16.111 --> 00:31:20.670
with a bottle of Jack
Daniels on the table. Right?

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:24.130
And so, so we were able to

00:31:24.130 --> 00:31:28.410
put him in a living room
environment by himself,

00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:33.410
bottle on the table, and
measure his ability to

00:31:34.153 --> 00:31:36.890
number one, use his coping skills

00:31:36.890 --> 00:31:38.470
'cause we can see his craving levels.

00:31:38.470 --> 00:31:41.754
We can see his, his stress
levels immediately elevate

00:31:41.754 --> 00:31:43.380
because he's by himself.

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:44.690
Now, we're totally silent.

00:31:44.690 --> 00:31:45.882
No one's saying anything.

00:31:45.882 --> 00:31:49.430
And your, your brain just
automatically goes into a mode

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:51.530
where you're, you just feel like,

00:31:51.530 --> 00:31:54.300
you know that you're
not in this environment.

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:56.430
You know that your
doctor is there with you

00:31:56.430 --> 00:31:57.770
and that this is a safe place,

00:31:57.770 --> 00:31:59.030
but it doesn't feel like it anymore

00:31:59.030 --> 00:32:00.823
because you don't see anyone there.

00:32:02.050 --> 00:32:04.409
And so you're in this alternate reality,

00:32:04.409 --> 00:32:08.340
we can measure the the
trigger, the response,

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:10.141
the craving levels, the stress.

00:32:10.141 --> 00:32:14.425
We stay silent and we see
how long it takes the patient

00:32:14.425 --> 00:32:17.446
to use their coping skills,
their grounding skills,

00:32:17.446 --> 00:32:21.190
all of the therapies that
they've been learning. Right?

00:32:21.190 --> 00:32:23.000
'Cause these are initially exercises,

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:24.299
but if you don't practice them,

00:32:24.299 --> 00:32:26.276
if I just do a couple of pushups,

00:32:26.276 --> 00:32:28.590
we're not going to expect
to be really strong. Right?

00:32:28.590 --> 00:32:30.140
I have to do it on a daily basis,

00:32:30.140 --> 00:32:33.000
and I have to practice when
my trainer's not there.

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:37.600
So by being able to put the
patient in that environment,

00:32:37.600 --> 00:32:40.922
stay silent and have them do
the therapies on their own,

00:32:40.922 --> 00:32:43.820
we can now, you know, monitor the patient.

00:32:43.820 --> 00:32:46.040
And when he's still, when he's,

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:48.010
let's say he's in level eight,

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:52.346
and he's, he gets himself down
with his grounding exercises

00:32:52.346 --> 00:32:54.400
to a level six.

00:32:54.400 --> 00:32:56.190
So now he's taking himself through

00:32:56.190 --> 00:32:57.560
without the help of the doctor.

00:32:57.560 --> 00:32:59.020
He's practicing.

00:32:59.020 --> 00:33:01.010
It's no longer a theory, right?

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:03.870
It's no longer, it
starts outside the room.

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:06.903
Now it literally starts in
the office with the doctor.

00:33:06.903 --> 00:33:11.450
And so when the doctor realizes
he's having trouble getting

00:33:11.450 --> 00:33:13.380
from a six lower,

00:33:13.380 --> 00:33:16.950
but he's gotten himself
from an eight to a six,

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:18.180
then the doctor will say, okay,

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:19.690
remember what we talked about.

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:20.780
Take a deep breath.

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:23.030
Remember the story you told
me about your grandmother

00:33:23.030 --> 00:33:23.863
in the park.

00:33:23.863 --> 00:33:25.076
Think of something positive.

00:33:25.076 --> 00:33:28.256
And so the doctor's
able to get him down to

00:33:28.256 --> 00:33:30.690
a level four. Right?

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:32.958
With her help talking him through it.

00:33:32.958 --> 00:33:37.059
The ability to be able to
show that data to insurance,

00:33:37.059 --> 00:33:39.510
we're gathering all of that information

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:43.200
because our goal is for
insurance to cover, you know,

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:45.230
six months of treatment for patients.

00:33:45.230 --> 00:33:46.430
But the only way-

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:47.570
- [Interviewer] The cost
of mental health is-

00:33:47.570 --> 00:33:48.415
- Absolutely.

00:33:48.415 --> 00:33:49.280
- [Interviewer] A big problem right?

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:51.800
- So the cost of, of real treatment

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:55.463
would be about, and
this is very low, right,

00:33:56.560 --> 00:34:00.270
would be about 250,000
for a year for somebody

00:34:00.270 --> 00:34:02.627
if you were to constantly go door to door.

00:34:02.627 --> 00:34:04.549
- [Interviewer] So, so you
have a scholarship program?

00:34:04.549 --> 00:34:08.170
- Yeah. So I'm actually starting
the scholarship program.

00:34:08.170 --> 00:34:10.910
I just registered a nonprofit called

00:34:10.910 --> 00:34:15.330
the Jevremovic Institute of
Behavior and Brain Sciences.

00:34:15.330 --> 00:34:17.360
And so the reason for that

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:19.787
is initially because I
work in mental health,

00:34:19.787 --> 00:34:23.220
my sisters have insurance and
our family is very supportive

00:34:23.220 --> 00:34:27.658
in working towards making sure
that they're always covered.

00:34:27.658 --> 00:34:30.190
The treatment centers that are my clients

00:34:30.190 --> 00:34:34.511
are beyond supportive of what
I'm doing and my situation,

00:34:34.511 --> 00:34:39.511
are willing to discount the
beds for, for my sisters to go.

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:44.940
And I know, I know the
industry inside and out

00:34:44.940 --> 00:34:47.410
when it comes to insurance and facilities

00:34:47.410 --> 00:34:49.930
and all of the politics behind it,

00:34:49.930 --> 00:34:52.092
and I'm still struggling
to find treatment.

00:34:52.092 --> 00:34:55.972
So to me, it's even unfathomable
to think of people that

00:34:55.972 --> 00:35:00.130
are uninsured or don't,
don't have any contacts

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:01.330
in the mental health industry.

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:04.060
Where would you even begin? Right?

00:35:04.060 --> 00:35:07.713
And so the, the reason for
the mental health scholarship,

00:35:07.713 --> 00:35:12.033
the JIBBS scholarship is
initially it costs about

00:35:12.033 --> 00:35:16.470
20, 250,000 conservatively
'cause we do get a lot

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:20.150
of discounted beds through my clients.

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:24.692
And so it's about offering
the ability for somebody.

00:35:24.692 --> 00:35:29.298
So every 250,000 cover
somebody for one year.

00:35:29.298 --> 00:35:32.390
It's about offering them
the ability to not just go

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:34.520
to a Medicaid Medi-Cal facility,

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:36.820
but they're able to
attend an Aura facility,

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:39.403
so treatment centers
that use our technology,

00:35:40.260 --> 00:35:43.925
receive three months of residential care,

00:35:43.925 --> 00:35:46.972
receive the detox before that,

00:35:46.972 --> 00:35:50.030
and then after the three
months of residential care,

00:35:50.030 --> 00:35:52.350
they're put into an outpatient program

00:35:52.350 --> 00:35:56.320
where they have, you know,
from 9:00 AM to 5:00 PM,

00:35:56.320 --> 00:36:00.000
they're, they're going to
therapy and doing treatment.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.173
And then they go home.

00:36:01.173 --> 00:36:03.350
So they have a little bit more freedom,

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:04.950
but the home is a sober living.

00:36:04.950 --> 00:36:07.890
And it's, there's a lot of
sketchy sober livings out there,

00:36:07.890 --> 00:36:09.300
but the ones that we work with

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:11.590
also have the Aura technology.

00:36:11.590 --> 00:36:15.730
And so you can continue to
practice what you were learning

00:36:15.730 --> 00:36:16.563
in the treatment center.

00:36:16.563 --> 00:36:18.920
So those gamified therapies that are fun

00:36:18.920 --> 00:36:20.633
but also retrain your brain.

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:26.446
And they also have supervision
at these sober livings,

00:36:26.446 --> 00:36:30.890
activities, they drive you
to and from the facilities

00:36:30.890 --> 00:36:32.730
for your therapy sessions.

00:36:32.730 --> 00:36:37.730
And then initially after,
after another six months,

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:39.410
so by month nine,

00:36:39.410 --> 00:36:40.950
you would begin job training,

00:36:40.950 --> 00:36:43.580
and we would cut down
your therapy sessions

00:36:43.580 --> 00:36:45.110
to the Aura mobile app.

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:48.950
So you do a couple hours
a week of tele-health,

00:36:48.950 --> 00:36:50.880
and we continue to track your progress.

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:53.473
So voice analysis, biofeedback

00:36:55.970 --> 00:36:59.250
and the evidence-based practices

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:02.900
through the Aura Guardian
system, which is our mobile app.

00:37:02.900 --> 00:37:07.900
And so train you for, for job placement

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:10.286
and ensure that you have the ability to

00:37:10.286 --> 00:37:12.612
move out of the sober living safely

00:37:12.612 --> 00:37:14.500
and continue on that path.

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:16.010
But after a year,

00:37:16.010 --> 00:37:19.100
we've completely reprogrammed
your behavior patterns,

